# What's inside the `<script>` section

Short answer: **one part of it really is encoded, and the rest only looks encoded because it's written on
extremely long lines.** Neither part is obfuscated or minified — it's ordinary JavaScript and ordinary data,
just formatted for compactness rather than for reading.

There are two different files involved, and they look strange for two different reasons.

---

## 1. The outer file (`index.html` / `HudsonCounty_AllYears.html`)

This file is only **81 lines long**. Eighty of them are perfectly readable. The problem is line 45:

```
line 45   9,471,041 characters   const MAPS={"jc": {"2013": "PCFET0NUWVBFIGh0bWw+CjxodG1sIGxhbmc9…
```

That single line is **9.4 million characters of Base64** — so yes, your instinct was right, this part is
genuinely encoded. Base64 is not encryption; it's a way of writing arbitrary text using only letters,
digits, `+` and `/`. Every browser can decode it in one function call, and so can you.

**What's encoded:** the ten complete year-pages — Jersey City 2013/2017/2021/2025 and Hoboken
2015/2017/2019/2021/2023/2025. Each is a whole standalone HTML document, encoded to a string and stored
in the `MAPS` object.

**Why:** you asked for single self-contained files. An HTML file can't contain another raw HTML document
inside a variable — the inner `</script>` and `</html>` tags would terminate the outer file early and
break it. Encoding sidesteps that. It also keeps each year isolated: they're loaded into an `<iframe>`,
so ten separate maps with identically-named variables can coexist without colliding.

**To see it for yourself,** paste this into your browser's console with the file open:

```js
// prints the Hoboken 2025 page as normal, readable HTML
console.log(b64ToStr(MAPS.hob['2025']))
```

or, to save it as its own file:

```js
const blob = new Blob([b64ToStr(MAPS.jc['2025'])], {type:'text/html'});
window.open(URL.createObjectURL(blob));   // opens that year as a standalone page
```

### The 35 readable lines

Everything after line 45 is the whole outer program:

| Lines | What it does |
|---|---|
| 46–47 | `YEARS` — which years each town has. `DEFAULT_YEAR` — where to start. |
| 48–49 | Grabs the iframe and the "Loading map…" element; remembers the current town/year. |
| 50 | `b64ToStr()` — the Base64 decoder. Three lines: decode, copy to a byte array, read as UTF-8. |
| 51–57 | `renderYears()` — draws the year buttons for the selected town and scrolls the active one into view. |
| 58–68 | `showMap()` — the core. Highlights the chosen buttons, then **line 67** decodes that year's page and hands it to the iframe via `srcdoc`. That one line is the whole "load a map" mechanism. |
| 69–73 | `pickTown()` — when you switch town, keep the same year if that town also has it, otherwise fall back to its default. |
| 74–77 | Wire up the buttons; hide the loading message when the frame finishes; open on Jersey City 2025. |

That's the entire outer file. It is a menu and a loader — it contains no mapping logic at all.

