## 2. Inside one year page

Decode any year and you get a normal HTML document — but it has the same "wall of characters" problem
for a *different* reason. For Jersey City 2025:

```
558 lines total
line 125    3,816,858 characters    const DATA = {"MAYOR_FEATS": [{"type":"Feature","geometry": …
```

This one is **not encoded** — it's plain JSON, just all on one line. It is **99.1% of the file**, and
it's entirely data, not instructions:

* the **boundary polygon of every voting district**, as long lists of longitude/latitude pairs
  (`[-74.10465, 40.71563], [-74.10471, 40.71559], …`) — this is what makes it so enormous; a single
  district outline can be hundreds of coordinate pairs
* the **vote count for every candidate in every district**, plus ballots cast and registered voters
* the **official summary totals** for each contest

The remaining **433 lines are the actual program**, and they're readable once indented. I've done that
for you — see `JerseyCity_2025_logic.js` and `Hoboken_2025_logic.js`, which are the real code with the
data blob removed, re-indented, and commented, with an index of every function at the top.

### How the program is organised

It's a small **state machine**. One object records what you're looking at:

```js
let state = { race:'mayor', ward:null, view:'margin', sub:'pct' };
```

Every button does the same two things: change one field in `state`, then call `draw()`. `draw()` rebuilds
the screen from scratch each time. There's no incremental updating, which is why the code stays simple.

The 433 lines fall into six groups:

**1. Colour maths (~60 lines).** `lerp3()` blends two colours. `accentT()` converts a vote share into a
blend amount on the deliberately non-linear curve we tuned. `RANKPAL` is the fifteen-colour palette —
this array is where 1st place orange, 2nd blue, 3rd magenta, 4th violet live. `computeRanks()` sorts
candidates by their official total and caches the result, which is what makes a candidate's colour
identical on the map, in the legend and in the results list.

**2. Map setup (~5 lines).** Creates the Leaflet map and adds the two CartoDB basemap layers.

**3. Reading the data (~30 lines).** Small helpers — `currentCands()`, `currentFeatures()`,
`currentSummary()` — that answer "given the current `state`, which slice of `DATA` do I need?" This is
where general-vs-runoff and citywide-vs-ward get resolved.

**4. Building the controls (~70 lines).** `raceButtons()`, `renderWardSeg()`, `renderViewSeg()`,
`renderSubSeg()` draw the button rows. `availableViews()` is the one with real logic: it decides that
Turnout only appears when registered-voter counts exist, and Runoff only when that contest had one.

**5. The small-multiples grid (~90 lines).** `featBBox()`, `pathFor()` and `centroidFor()` project the
district polygons into hand-drawn SVG (Leaflet isn't used here — these are tiny static maps).
`buildGrid()` assembles one mini-map per candidate.

**6. Drawing (~180 lines).** `draw()` is the conductor. `styleFor()` picks each district's fill colour
for the active view; `tooltipFor()` builds the hover box; `updateHeader()`, `updateLegend()` and
`updateSummary()` refresh the three text panels.

